fix(db): T-403 — rename the fetch-state ENUM; it collided with the table name

BLOCKER, found by applying 0035 to an empty database. Migration 0035 created an
ENUM named `document_fetch_state` AND a TABLE named `document_fetch_state`.
Postgres creates an implicit composite type for every table, so the second
CREATE is refused:

    error: type "document_fetch_state" already exists   (SQLSTATE 42710)

Reproduced here on ipodhan_test: `drizzle-kit migrate` exited 1 mid-journal.
In production this would have failed the deploy at the migrate step, and
assert-migrations-applied.sh would have blocked the release — the first symptom
would have been a failed deploy, not a test.

Fix: the enum is now `document_fetch_status`; the TABLE keeps its name. Renamed
in schema.ts (documentFetchStatusEnum) and in the journaled 0035 file, which has
never been applied anywhere, so hand-editing it is allowed and the journal entry
is unchanged. Both places carry a comment explaining why the names must differ.

PROOF (evidence/T-403/migration-readback.json): the full journal now replays on
an empty database, exit 0 — 19 of 19 journal entries applied, 17 tables created,
document_fetch_state has all 16 columns with `state` typed document_fetch_status,
all four indexes plus unique_doc_fetch_state_per_ipo_type present, document_type
carries the three new values, and ipos has bse_ipo_no and
bse_payload_lead_manager_count.

GUARD so the class cannot recur: scripts/lib/migration-name-collision.mjs plus a
node self-test wired into pr-gate.yml. It parses EVERY journaled migration and
fails when any CREATE TYPE name equals any CREATE TABLE name — including the
harder case where the enum lands in one release and the table in a later one.
Verified against the original 0035: the guard catches it.
